<header>
  <h1>The sales effect of "Free App of the Day"<br>on Amazon Appstore: An empirical study</h1>
  <h2>Hong Chen, Kevin Lachaud, Wenqi Zhou</h2>
</header>

<section class="intro-section">
  <div class="intro-text">
    <h3 class="section-title">Introduction</h3>
    <p>Apps, as an abbreviation of mobile applications, are software products for mobile platforms (Tang, 2016). They are designed to perform certain tasks on mobile devices (Islam, Islam, &amp; Mazumder, 2010). This product category has grown to become a gigantic differentiated market with over 3.48 million apps on Google Play, an app marketplace, and the recent projection of over 935-billion-dollar revenue in 2023 (Statista Research, 2021a, Statista Research, 2021b).</p>

    <h3 class="section-title" style="margin-top:30px;">Objective</h3>
    <p>This work studies the adverse effect of "Free App of the Day" at Amazon Appstore.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="intro-image">
    <div class="placeholder">[Photo: hand holding smartphone with smartwatch, top-right]</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="main-section">
  <div class="columns">
    <div class="col-left">
      <h3 class="section-title">Methodology</h3>

      <p><strong class="bold-item">1. Literature review</strong></p>
      <p class="indent-a">a. Digital free sampling</p>
      <p class="indent-a">b. eWOM</p>

      <p><strong class="bold-item">2. Hypotheses</strong></p>
      <p class="indent-a">a. H1 - Online user decisions to adopt apps are discouraged by the price rebound after the zero-price offering of FAD ends. The sharper price rebound leads to a greater decrease in app adoptions.</p>
      <p class="indent-a">b. H2 - Conditional on the total volume of eWOM, organic reviews are more influential to online user decisions of adopting apps than promotion-induced reviews after the FAD promotion ends.</p>

      <p><strong class="bold-item">3. Data</strong></p>
      <p class="indent-a">a. Context and data collection</p>
      <p class="indent-a">b. Key variables</p>

      <p><strong class="bold-item">4. Empirical analysis</strong></p>
      <p class="indent-a">a. Exploratory analysis</p>
      <p class="indent-a">b. Regression model and results</p>

      <h3 class="section-title" style="margin-top:30px;">Conclusion</h3>
      <p>We conclude that these promotion-induced reviews are less influential than organic reviews.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col-right">
      <h3 class="section-title">Results</h3>
      <p>We notice that a few apps were off the direct spotlight post-promotion; however, they still offer zero price for another one or two days.</p>
      <p>Specifically, an increase in SkewDaily<sub>i</sub> indicates a more right skewed distribution of daily number of reviews during the post-promotion period.</p>

